      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p10-q8-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "No. Exactly 8 of the 16 sign choices factorise into nonconstant real polynomials. A counterexample is ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "x^2+x-xy+y",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ".",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p10-q8-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Grouping and comparing coefficients gives the condition ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "AD=BC",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " for ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "Ax^2+Bx+Cxy+Dy",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ", where each coefficient is ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\pm1",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ".",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "No. Interpret factorisation as a product of nonconstant real polynomials.\n\nWrite the expression as $Ax^2+Bx+Cxy+Dy$, where each coefficient is $+1$ or $-1$. Grouping gives\n$$Ax^2+Bx+Cxy+Dy=x(Ax+B)+y(Cx+D).$$\nIf $AD=BC$, the brackets are proportional, so\n$$Ax^2+Bx+Cxy+Dy=(Ax+B)\\left(x+\\frac{C}{A}y\\right).$$\nTo see why this condition is necessary, a factorisation of this quadratic must have two linear factors. The missing $y^2$ term means one factor has no $y$ term. The missing constant term, together with the nonzero $y$ term, then gives the form $(ux+v)(wx+zy)$. Comparing coefficients gives\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=uw\\\\B&=vw\\\\C&=uz\\\\D&=vz\\\\AD&=(uw)(vz)\\\\AD&=(vw)(uz)\\\\AD&=BC.\\end{align*}$$\nFor each of the $2^3=8$ choices of $A,B,C$, exactly one sign $D=\\frac{BC}{A}$ works. Thus exactly $8$ of the $16$ choices factorise.\n\nFor example, choose $+,+,-,+$, giving $x^2+x-xy+y$. Here $AD=1$ and $BC=-1$, so it cannot factorise into nonconstant real polynomials. Bob's claim is false.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
